<div class="page-heading">
    <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">HELP · MEETING REVIEW</p>
        <h1>How review works</h1>
        <p>Choose your location, confirm meeting intent and review exact times before approval.</p>
    </div>
</div>
<div class="help-grid">
    <section class="panel help-card">
        <h2>Start with a community</h2>
        <p>
            Your location offers communities in British Columbia, Alberta, Saskatchewan and Manitoba.
            Choose manually and explicitly confirm before Meetings opens. No location is detected.
        </p>
        <p>
            Changing your community clears all review decisions and asks for confirmation again.
        </p>
    </section>
    <section class="panel help-card">
        <h2>One list, three approaches</h2>
        <p>
            Meetings contains six organizer meetings starting November 9–13, 2026.
            Search and filters do not change the full selection scope.
            Select all includes all five supported meetings, including hidden matches.
        </p>
        <p>
            Guided review asks you to choose a time for each meeting. Express selection supplies a local-time target for undecided meetings.
            All approaches share selection, preview and approval.
        </p>
    </section>
    <section class="panel help-card">
        <h2>Assistance only when you ask</h2>
        <p>
            Meetings start as Not assessed. Request rule-based suggestions to see proposed local-time choices, no-change recommendations and unresolved or unsupported meetings.
            Applying local-time suggestions fills untouched meetings only and never replaces manual decisions.
        </p>
        <p>
            Manage review suggestions and check connection information in Account &amp; AI.
            Turning suggestions on or off resets review decisions. Manual review remains available.
        </p>
    </section>
    <section class="panel help-card">
        <h2>Exact review before simulation</h2>
        <p>
            Selecting a meeting and choosing its time are separate decisions.
            Only selected, supported meetings with a local-time or worldwide-time choice enter preview.
            Unresolved selections block approval. Skip removes a meeting from the batch.
        </p>
        <p>
            Final review shows exact local and UTC starts and ends. The weekly series covers November 10, 17 and 24 only.
            Complex recurrence is excluded. Notification effects are unknown and not simulated.
        </p>
    </section>
    <section class="panel help-card">
        <h2>Your choices stay in this session</h2>
        <p>
            There is no permanent save or history. A new session or reset starts fresh.
            Selection, intent, location, analysis and assistance changes clear stale previews and simulation results.
            Switching modes preserves your choices. Reopening an unchanged preview preserves its version and any simulation results.
        </p>
        <p>Approval simulates only the exact batch shown. No provider request, invitation, retry or revert happens.</p>
    </section>
    <section class="panel help-card">
        <h2>Which time should I choose?</h2>
        <p>
            “Keep my local time” preserves the source clock time in your selected community using the comparison offset.
            “Keep the same worldwide moment” keeps the stored UTC time and may show a different local time.
        </p>
        <p>
            A title or UTC timestamp cannot establish your intent. Choose “I'm not sure yet” when you need more context.
            Neither uncertainty nor an unsupported series is treated as a recommendation to change.
        </p>
    </section>
</div>
<div class="detail-actions">
    <button class="primary" @onclick="OnExplore">Choose location <AppIcon Name="AppIconName.Next" /></button>
    <button class="secondary" @onclick="OnReset">Reset workspace</button>
</div>

@code {
    [Parameter] public EventCallback OnExplore { get; set; }
    [Parameter] public EventCallback OnReset { get; set; }
}
